An interface is a blueprint of a class that contains static constants and abstract methods.
Interfaces are used to achieve abstraction.
An interface contains only abstract methods (methods without a body) and variables. 
It cannot be instantiated, similar to an abstract class, and represents an IS-A relationship.

Why Interface?
| Problem                        | How Interface Helps           |
| ------------------------------ | ----------------------------- |
| Tight coupling                 | Program to abstractions       |
| Multiple inheritance ambiguity | Solved via interfaces         |
| Changing implementations       | Swap without touching callers |
| Unit testing                   | Easy mocking                  |
| Spring DI                      | Preferred injection point     |

3. Interface Syntax (All Possibilities)
  public interface MyInterface {
  
      // Constant (implicitly public static final)
      int MAX = 100;
  
      // Abstract method (implicitly public abstract)
      void doWork();
  
      // Default method (Java 8+)
      default void log() {
          System.out.println("Logging");
      }
  
      // Static method (Java 8+)
      static int add(int a, int b) {
          return a + b;
      }
  
      // Private helper (Java 9+)
      private void helper() {
          // internal reuse
      }
  }

4. Key Rules (Must-Know)
  Methods are public by default
  Variables are public static final
  No constructors
  Cannot be instantiated
  A class can implement multiple interfaces
  An interface can extend multiple interfaces

5. Interface vs Abstract Class (🔥 Very Important)
| Feature               | Interface                 | Abstract Class      |
| --------------------- | ------------------------- | ------------------- |
| Multiple inheritance  | ✅ Yes                     | ❌ No                |
| State (instance vars) | ❌ No                      | ✅ Yes               |
| Constructors          | ❌ No                      | ✅ Yes               |
| Methods               | abstract, default, static | abstract + concrete |
| Variables             | public static final       | any                 |
| Spring preference     | High                      | Medium              |

6. Default Methods (Java 8+)
Why?
To add new methods without breaking existing implementations.
  
  interface Vehicle {
      void start();
      default void fuelType() {
          System.out.println("Petrol/Diesel");
      }
  }
  //Overriding Default Method
  class Car implements Vehicle {
      public void start() { }
      @Override
      public void fuelType() {
          System.out.println("Petrol");
      }
  }

7. Static Methods in Interface:
Belong to the interface
Cannot be overridden
  interface MathUtil {
      static int add(int a, int b) {
          return a + b;
      }
  }
  // Usage
  int s = MathUtil.add(10, 20);

8. Multiple Inheritance via Interface (Key Topic)
    Problem with classes
    class A { void show(){} }
    class B { void show(){} }
    // class C extends A, B ❌
    
    Solution with interface
    interface A { void show(); }
    interface B { void show(); }
    
    class C implements A, B {
        public void show() {
            System.out.println("Resolved");
        }
    }
9. Diamond Problem with Default Methods
interface A {
    default void msg(){ System.out.println("A"); }
}
interface B {
    default void msg(){ System.out.println("B"); }
}
class C implements A, B {
    @Override
    public void msg() {
        A.super.msg(); // or B.super.msg()
    }
}

Rule: If two interfaces provide the same default method, the class must override it.

10. Functional Interface (🔥 Very Important)
Definition
Exactly one abstract method.

@FunctionalInterface
interface Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b);
}

Lambda
    Calculator c = (a, b) -> a + b;

Common Functional Interfaces
    Predicate<T> → test
    Function<T,R> → apply
    Consumer<T> → accept
    Supplier<T> → get
    Runnable, Callable

11. Marker Interface
    No methods—used for metadata.
    interface Serializable {}
    interface Cloneable {}
    JVM/framework provides special behavior based on presence.
    
12. Interface Variables (Tricky)
  interface Test {
      int x = 10;
  }
  Equivalent to:
  public static final int x = 10;
  ❌ Cannot change
  ❌ No instance variables

13. Interface & Encapsulation
  Interface does not expose data
  Exposes behavior only
  Implementation details remain hidden

interface Bank {
    double getInterest();
}

14. Interface in Spring Boot (🔥 Critical)
Service Contract
public interface PaymentService {
    void pay();
}

@Service
public class CreditCardPayment implements PaymentService {
    public void pay() { }
}

Why Spring prefers interfaces?
    Loose coupling
    Easy mocking (@MockBean)
    Cleaner architecture
    JDK proxies for AOP

